ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need a RISC-V "newlib" tool chain from
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain, and qemu compiled for
riscv64-softmmu.  Once they are installed, and in your shell
search path, you can run "make qemu".

ESP32-S3 (ESP-IDF 6.0) M1 PORT

This repository also contains a first ESP-IDF based bring-up path for
ESP32-S3 (`main/app_main.c` with code in `kernel/*_esp*.c`).

Key properties of this M1 bring-up:
- single app task runtime (`app_main`),
- UART interactive kernel shell (`ksh`),
- raw SPI flash partition block backend (`xv6fs`).

Build steps:
1. Install ESP-IDF 6.0 and export the environment.
2. Set target: `idf.py set-target esp32s3`
3. Build: `idf.py build`
4. Flash and monitor: `idf.py -p <PORT> flash monitor`

Filesystem image source:
- Everything under `fsroot/` is packed into `xv6fs.bin` during build.
- ELF user commands are built and copied into `fsroot/bin` in the staging step.

ESP QEMU smoke test:
- `./scripts/qemu_smoke_esp.py`
- `idf.py qemu_smoke`

Includes PTY check via `ptydemo` (`/dev/ptmx` <-> dynamic `/dev/pts/N`).
Also checks two-ELF PTY flow: `ptysend` -> `ptyrecv`.
Shell supports background jobs: `cmd &`, `ps`, `jobs`, `wait [jobid]`.

The ESP build is intentionally separate from the original `make qemu`
flow and does not replace the RISC-V path.
